# Portfolio Standards

## TypeScript

- Keep TypeScript strict mode enabled (`strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` where practical).
- Avoid `any`. Prefer Zod-inferred types and generated Next.js types.
- Validate project content at build time; fail with useful errors when required fields are missing.

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML landmarks (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `footer`, `section`).
- One logical `h1` per page; preserve heading order.
- Provide skip-to-content, visible focus, keyboard operability, and Escape-to-close for overlays.
- Support `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Target WCAG 2.2 AA contrast and interaction patterns.
- Charts and evidence images need short alt text plus visible captions or data tables when values matter.
- Never rely on color alone to communicate meaning.

## Components and architecture

- Prefer reusable components under `src/components/`.
- Use React Server Components by default; Client Components only for interaction.
- Keep dependencies minimal. Do not add CMS, auth, database, Redux, or decorative chart libraries.

## Content integrity

- Never invent project results, metrics, employer relationships, clients, awards, or traction numbers.
- Never invent confidential or proprietary data.
- Missing metrics must remain null and must not render empty KPI cards.
- Prefer clearly labeled placeholders over fabricated evidence.
- Founder/DigiPae content supports analyst positioning; do not turn pages into a startup pitch.

## Writing

- Recruiter-friendly: business outcomes before technical detail.
- Active voice, short paragraphs, precise responsibility language.
- Distinguish findings, impact, and recommendations.
- For independent/synthetic work use: Demonstrated, Identified, Modeled, Evaluated, Designed, Recommended, Simulated.

## Responsive design

- Mobile-first layout; no horizontal page scroll.
- Title map becomes a card grid on small screens.
- Test common breakpoints before completion.

## Validation before completion

- Run lint, TypeScript checks, production build, Playwright smoke tests, and accessibility checks.
- Do not suppress legitimate errors to force a green build.
- Document missing assets and unverified content.

## Documentation

- Keep README, content-authoring, evidence checklist, and Netlify deployment docs accurate.
- Do not claim the Netlify contact form works until verified in a deployed Netlify preview.
